Ladybird leaves the fills and strokes of an inline SVG as authored
under force dark when its outermost svg element’s border box is wider
or taller than 64 CSS pixels, and darkens them as SVG content when it
fits within 64x64. Blink does the same, in SVGRootPainter::
PaintReplaced(): ImageClassifierHelper::GetSVGDocumentType() takes an
SVG root wider or taller than kMaxImageLength (64), after undoing the
layout zoom, as kPhoto, and a GraphicsContext::ScopedAutoDarkModeState
then pauses dark mode for its content, behind the runtime feature
AutoDarkModeSVGSizeThreshold (status stable). Run in Chrome
158.0.8127.37 with WebContentsForceDark, an inline SVG with a black
rect inverted at 60 and 64px and stayed black at 100, 120, 140, 160 and
300px, and at 65x20 and 20x65. A 60px SVG with 4px of padding (a 68px
border box) stayed black, and so did a 40px svg element nested in a
200x50 one. The 160px QR code on discord.com rendered as authored.

Ladybird differs from Blink in two cases, both left for later. Going
by its source (not run), Blink’s pause also reaches HTML content in a
foreignObject of a large SVG root, which Ladybird still darkens. And
Blink applies the rule to the root of an SVG document too: Chrome left
a 135x40 SVG badge image and a 200x50 SVG wordmark image as authored,
where Ladybird darkens the content of SVG images, and of an SVG document
opened on its own, whatever their size. Gecko and WebKit have no
force-dark mode.
